// shopify domain -- thin product index (memql#4137), and MemQL's first
// declared MIRROR (epic memql#4378).
//
// Shopify writes, MemQL reads -- and since memql#4378 that sentence is
// enforced rather than intended: @origin("shopify") makes the concept
// read-only by construction, so a user, an agent, a tool handler or a
// raw insert aimed at it is refused with mirror_write_refused{shopify}. The row is a projection of three
// Storefront fields: GID (the row id), handle, availableForSale.
// Images, collections, prices, and inventory counts are NOT stored.
// Merchandising still comes from the Storefront API via
// integration.shopify.fetchProduct.
//
// A missing Shopify product is never invented: upsert only after a
// successful Storefront/Admin fetch. A delete webhook or a reconcile
// miss retires a row we already had; it does not create one.

/// Thin Shopify product projection. Row id IS the product GID
/// (gid://shopify/Product/...). Three index fields only. clusterOwner:
/// one catalog per deployment, readable for campaigns/reviews/agents.
/// A MIRROR: Shopify is the origin, so this row is read-only in MemQL
/// by construction -- only the shopify connector writes it, under its
/// own actor. Change a product in Shopify; MemQL's copy follows.
@origin("shopify")
@displayCard(primary="handle", secondary="availableForSale", status="present")
@rowAuthz(clusterOwner)
concept shopifyProduct {
  handle            string!  @description("Storefront handle. The only merchandising key we keep; collections and images stay on Shopify.")
  availableForSale  bool!    @description("Storefront availableForSale at last successful fetch. Not an inventory count.")
  present           bool!    @description("False when the last fetch or a products/delete webhook said the product is gone. A missing product is retired, never invented.")
}
